Material for the tests of RSA and ECDSA verification, made once with openssl:
an RSA-2048 CA and a P-256 CA, each self-signed with SHA-256; an Ed25519 leaf
for `localhost` signed by each; the RSA CA's public key; and, over `msg`, an
RSA-PSS signature (SHA-256, salt 32) and an ECDSA signature (SHA-256, DER).
The keys are not here; nothing depends on them.

Added for the review (0.67.0), all Ed25519, made with openssl: `nc-ca.crt`,
a self-signed CA with a critical name constraint (the parser refuses it);
`client-only.crt`, a self-signed leaf for `localhost` whose extended key
usage is clientAuth alone; and `root2.crt` -> `inter-nosign.crt` (a CA whose
key usage is digitalSignature only) -> `leaf-by-inter.crt` (serverAuth,
`localhost`), a chain that must not link through the intermediate.

Added for P-384 and SHA-384 (0.89.0), made with openssl: `ec384-ca.crt`, a
P-384 CA self-signed with SHA-384, and `leaf-by-ec384.crt`, an Ed25519 leaf
for `localhost` it signed with ecdsa-with-SHA384; `ec-sha384-ca.crt` and
`leaf-by-ec-sha384.crt`, the same with a P-256 CA signing with SHA-384;
`rsa384-ca.crt` and `leaf-by-rsa384.crt`, an RSA-2048 CA and its leaf with
sha384WithRSAEncryption, and `rsa384-pub.pem`, its public key; over `msg`,
`ecdsa384.sig.b64` (the P-384 key, SHA-384, DER) and `pss384.sig.b64` (the
RSA key, PSS with SHA-384 and a 48-byte salt). The keys are not here.
